  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1153 PM CDT WED OCT 14 2026\n\nThis evening we have a surface boundary extending from the TX\nPanhandle, to AR, and NEWD to MD. The W portions of this boundary\nwill get pushed NWD as a warm front as we head into the day\nThursday as a cold front develops on the lee of the Rockies and\nthe associated low moves NEWD. By Friday morning this low is\nexpected to have progressed to the Great Lakes and combined with\nanother system pushing SEWD out of Central Canada. During Friday\nthis system should get enough of a favorable push from the upper\nflow to move through C AL and finally allow fall to make its\narrival in the area. There may be just enough moisture with the\nhelp of frontal forcing to allow for a few isolated showers Friday\nafternoon. No TS or heavy rain is anticipated. \n\nHowever, before that we still have Thu where we could see a few\nmax temp records in jeopardy (see climate section), and Fri where\nmax temps will still be above normal. Breezy and gusty at times\nNRLY winds should be felt Fri afternoon, that night and on into\nSaturday with tight surface pressure gradients behind the front. \nCool readings with lows in the 40s for many on Fri night and for\nall Sat/Sun nights with highs generally in the 60s over the\nweekend. Our airmass should begin to moderate for next week as our\nflow becomes more ERLY, but overall still dry. \n\n08",
